 ### Design concept
 
-The Host half watches each package's stamped entry artifact and serves `/plugins/events`. It forwards existing graph-change and rebuilt notifications; every new connection receives the current full graph. A graph describes the browser’s desired entries and carries no Host cleanup-completion guarantee. Host activation and cleanup remain owned by the Host lifecycle. The entry's mtime, ctime, and size identify the revision without hashing its contents; unchanged metadata requires no content read. Host restarts over unchanged artifacts keep the same revisions, so reconnecting the graph stream does not replace browser plugins. The browser half delegates both frame kinds to Client Modules, which serializes entry changes and waits for browser resource cleanup.
+The Host half watches each package's stamped entry artifact and serves `/plugins/events`. It forwards existing graph-change and rebuilt notifications; every new connection receives the current full graph. A graph describes the browser’s desired entries and carries no Host cleanup-completion guarantee. Host activation and cleanup remain owned by the Host lifecycle. The entry's mtime, ctime, and size identify the revision without hashing its contents; unchanged metadata requires no content read. Host restarts over unchanged artifacts keep the same revisions, so reconnecting the graph stream does not replace browser plugins. When a carrier publishes a Host stream origin, the browser connects there directly instead of proxying long-lived SSE through a custom document scheme. The browser half delegates both frame kinds to Client Modules, which serializes entry changes and waits for browser resource cleanup.
 
